          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "What is the maximum possible range for this set of data if there are no outliers?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$24$",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\mathrm{IQR} &= Q_U-Q_L\\\\\\mathrm{IQR} &= 16-10\\\\\\mathrm{IQR} &= 6\\end{align*}$$\nWith no outliers, the minimum and maximum must lie within the outlier boundaries.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Lower bound} &= Q_L-1.5\\times\\mathrm{IQR}\\\\\\text{Lower bound} &= 10-1.5(6)\\\\\\text{Lower bound} &= 10-9\\\\\\text{Lower bound} &= 1\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Upper bound} &= Q_U+1.5\\times\\mathrm{IQR}\\\\\\text{Upper bound} &= 16+1.5(6)\\\\\\text{Upper bound} &= 16+9\\\\\\text{Upper bound} &= 25\\end{align*}$$\nTo maximise the range, the minimum can be $1$ and the maximum can be $25$. Values equal to the boundaries are not outliers.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Maximum range} &= 25-1\\\\\\text{Maximum range} &= 24\\end{align*}$$\nThe maximum possible range is $24$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
